96875, 0.026580810546875, 0.0299224853515625, -0.004428863525390625, 0.000698089599609375, -0.0199432373046875, 0.02215576171875, 0.0029048919677734375, -0.009674072265625, 0.037506103515625, -0.0240936279296875, 0.0222320556640625, 0.00980377197265625, 0.0092315673828125, 0.01161956787109375, 0.00038313865661621094, 0.0404052734375, -0.006908416748046875, 0.05560302734375, -0.0015745162963867188, 0.03265380859375, -0.0069580078125, 0.0026340484619140625, 0.0087890625, -0.01045989990234375, 0.0009698867797851562, 0.00591278076171875, -0.0197906494140625, 0.0196533203125, 0.0511474609375, -0.023895263671875, -0.018768310546875, -0.0221710205078125, 0.0267486572265625, -0.01448822021484375, 0.00327301025390625, 0.04974365234375, 0.024658203125, -0.00710296630859375, -0.036102294921875, -0.01404571533203125, -0.0018739700317382812, -0.0132293701171875, -0.0322265625, 0.017608642578125, -0.03302001953125, -0.0128021240234375, -0.0057220458984375, -0.006988525390625, 0.020355224609375, 0.0178680419921875, -0.00211334228515625, -0.021636962890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, without considering the relationships between different generative tasks. A central challenge is not only how to curate each task-specific corpus, but also how to organize heterogeneous supervision according to the dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\nTo address this, we introduce a capability-driven data infrastructure\u2014a framework that systematically organizes data collection and training according to the progression of model capabilities. In this context, a \"capability\" refers to a distinct generative skill, such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, or associating images with external knowledge. Our infrastructure couples the construction of supervision for each capability with a curriculum that schedules training data in alignment with the dependencies among these capabilities. For example, text-to-image generation forms the foundation, upon which more complex tasks like image editing or knowledge association can build.\n\nThe infrastructure is built around three specialized yet interoperable data engines, each responsible for a specific type of supervision:\n1. The text-image grounding engine collects and curates high-quality image-caption pairs for text-to-image (T2I) generation, ensuring diverse and accurate textual descriptions.\n2. The inter-image transformation engine constructs paired examples for image editing tasks, such as object addition, removal, or style transfer, by generating or mining before-and-after image pairs with corresponding transformation descriptions.\n3. The image-knowledge association engine links images to structured knowledge entities, enabling models to connect visual content with external information such as object categories or factual attributes.\n\nThese engines operate in tandem, with \"caption experts\" aligning supervision across tasks and granularities. For instance, captions generated or refined for T2I can be adapted to describe editing transformations, ensuring consistency and interoperability across datasets.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum guides the training process. It jointly evolves task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution, following the dependency order of capability acquisition. Early stages focus on foundational tasks like T2I, while later stages introduce more complex editing and knowledge association tasks. This curriculum is dynamically adjusted based on ongoing evaluation.\n\nCapability-aware evaluation closes the loop by identifying gaps in model performance and informing data resampling. This process involves three main steps: (1) targeted retrieval of challenging or underrepresented examples, (2) expert construction of new or improved supervision for these cases, and (3) gap-aware resampling to rebalance the training data. This iterative process ensures that the dataset remains comprehensive and well-aligned with the evolving capabilities of the model.\n\nFor data curation and quality assurance, we apply automated filtering to remove low-quality or irrelevant images and captions, use human and model-based validation to assess annotation accuracy, and employ rebalancing strategies to ensure diverse coverage of visual concepts and tasks. Recaptioning and expert review further enhance data quality, especially for complex editing and knowledge association examples.\n\nAt scale, our framework curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. We use these datasets to train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two parameter scales: 3 billion (3B) and 6 billion (6B). The models are trained using standard diffusion architectures with cross-modal attention, and the training protocol includes staged curriculum learning as described above. For evaluation, we compare our models against established baselines on CPI-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark for compositional and photorealistic image generation.\n\nQuantitative evaluation uses metrics such as FID (Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance), CLIP score, and retrieval accuracy to assess image quality, text-image alignment, and knowledge association. Qualitative evaluation involves human assessment of generated images across diverse text-to-image and editing scenarios, focusing on criteria such as visual coverage (the range of concepts and scenes the model can depict), rendering versatility (the ability to handle varied styles and transformations), and effective transfer across generative capabilities (the model's performance on tasks beyond its primary training objective).\n\nExperimental results demonstrate that our approach achieves broad visual coverage, as measured by high diversity and accuracy across benchmark categories, versatile rendering in both photorealistic and stylized outputs, and effective transfer across generative capabilities, as evidenced by strong performance on both T2I and editing tasks."}}